@page rh="THE HOUSE OF VANE · DORIAN · STANDING" m="TKC: TL · CR · DOM · MEN · FR 37–38; VOL. I · {v1:crown} · {v1:crownward2}"
# The public version and the private one
rec:
In public | Handsome where Crest was cold, and everything about him is performance. Under him the Broadcast became genuinely sophisticated (TL). He holds the Quarterly Audiences where everyone watches the seating chart before they watch him (CR).
In private | Cold. The gap between his public and private selves is where his real decisions happen (CR).
On Judah | He's pressed Judah to choose an Anointed Wife since Judah turned twenty-three. He calls it duty, and it's also control, because an unmarried heir's loyalties are still unwritten (CR). The Trials exist partly to make Judah's choice feel like honor instead of pressure (MEN).
On his uncle | Crest was aware and didn't act. He neither arranged the removal nor prevented it, and he spent twenty-four years on a throne he knew the shape of (FR 37). Dorian was spared because a child's claim is a claim you can supervise (FR 38).
His appointments | Pacific's Governor-Prelate, who performs warmth while administering iron (DOM).
prompt: What he's like at a private dinner with Lyris when no one's serving | fill

@page rh="THE HOUSE OF VANE · DORIAN · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE · 1 OF 2" m="TKC: CR · TL · FR 44; VOL. I · {v1:teaches} · {v1:texts} · {v1:crown}"
# What he believes
rec:
What he believes | INFERENCE: In the machine, and in being loved by the people who live inside it (TL). OPEN QUESTION: In God?
What he knows | The ledger names Elias. He's known since 2061, and it's the only thing he's ever been certain of (FR 44).
Who taught him | Elias's house, then Crest's. INFERENCE: Two teachers, one who believed and one who administered (Vol. I, {v1:crown}).
What he'd be arrested for | INFERENCE: Nothing. He's the one who decides who is.
empire: What the Temple teaches about a man like him
The Vane bloodline carries divine sanction. God chose Elias, and the anointing passes to his descendants (CR). INTERPRETATION: The Temple's doctrine of the bloodline makes Dorian sacred, and his own knowledge of 2061 makes him the one man in the Empire who can't believe it.
/end
prompt: What he says when he prays in public, and whether he ever prays in private | fill

@page rh="THE HOUSE OF VANE · DORIAN · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE · 2 OF 2" m="2 SAM 9.7–13; DAN 3.4–6; JER 36.21–23; 2 SAM 7.16; DEUT 17.18–20; 2 SAM 12.7"
# The text
rec:
His name | *Dorian* has no biblical source. It's Greek, from the Dorians. INTERPRETATION: To most English readers it's Wilde's Dorian Gray, the beautiful man whose portrait ages in his place.
Mephibosheth · 2 Samuel 9 | POSSIBLE: The grandson of the fallen king, brought to David's court to "eat bread at my table continually." Kindness, and supervision. **Where it breaks:** Mephibosheth never takes the throne. The supervised child became the Emperor (FR 38).
The golden image · Daniel 3 | POSSIBLE: "At what time ye hear the sound of the cornet… ye fall down and worship." A king who governs by spectacle and music. **Where it breaks:** Nebuchadnezzar has a furnace for the ones who won't bow. Dorian's method is to make them love it (TL).
Jehoiakim · Jeremiah 36 | POSSIBLE: A king who cuts a prophet's scroll with a penknife, column by column, and burns it. **Where it breaks:** Jehoiakim destroys the document in front of his court. Dorian says put it back, and takes it later (FR 42–43).
tab:
